One TypeScript source, three runtimes. `@pancake/stack`
([`mod.ts`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/stack/mod.ts))
gives Deno and Bun the `Session` / `Server` API over the pancake C ABI, plus the
optional `@pancake/iroh` transport that `connect` loads lazily when it sees an
iroh locator.

The same source also compiles to WebAssembly as `@pancake/web`, so the same
`Session` runs in the browser, dialing rather than listening.
[In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')) covers how a page reaches its
peers.

Build the archives with `zig build`, and add `zig build iroh-abi` for the iroh
snippet.
Set `PANCAKE_LIB_DIR`, then run with `deno run --allow-ffi --allow-read`, adding
`--allow-net` and `--allow-env` as needed. One thing the snippets do not show:
`@pancake/stack` is a workspace member of the checkout, not a published
package, so run from `bindings/ts` or point an import map at
`bindings/ts/stack/mod.ts`. Install details:
[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')).

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip (connect, fetch, send,
handle the reply) reads like this in TypeScript. The inbound loop delivers the
partner's replies and dispatches its calls to the actors you publish. `fetch`
and `send` start it on demand, so a pure caller never calls `serve`; the example
holds `serve`'s promise anyway, to observe a clean or faulty shutdown.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { connect, tcpLocator } from "@pancake/stack";
>
> // tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> const partner = tcpLocator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> const session = await connect(partner);
> session.serve().catch(() => {});
>
> const greeter = await session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> try {
>   const reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
>   console.log(reply);
> } catch (reason) {
>   console.error("the greeter refused:", reason);
> }
>
> session.close();
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { connect } from "@pancake/stack";
> import { Reachability } from "@pancake/iroh";
>
> // iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. Discovery resolves
> // the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> const partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh";
>
> const session = await connect(partner, Reachability.Discovery);
> session.serve().catch(() => {});
>
> const greeter = await session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> try {
>   const reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
>   console.log(reply);
> } catch (reason) {
>   console.error("the greeter refused:", reason);
> }
>
> session.close();
> ```

The iroh snippet differs only in the locator: a bare node id plus the
`Reachability.Discovery` posture you passed to `connect`. `fetch` and `send` are
unchanged. For dial-by-id and discovery, see [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')).

The `serve` promise resolves when the session closes cleanly and rejects on a
transport or protocol error. When the loop ends, every `send` still awaiting a
reply breaks too. Failures surface on the calls you await; the bare `catch`
only keeps the loop's own rejection from going unhandled.

## Hosting the other half

The hosting side reads the same way. `listen` binds a port and returns a
`Server` you iterate for each accepted `Session`, `publish` makes an actor
fetchable by name, and `serve` dispatches the partner's calls to it.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { listen } from "@pancake/stack";
>
> // tcp: bind a place you chose, a host and port.
> const server = listen("127.0.0.1", 9099);
> for await (const session of server) {
>   session.publish("greeter", (_verb, args) => `hello, ${args[0]}`);
>   session.serve().catch(() => {});
> }
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { listenIroh, Reachability } from "@pancake/iroh";
>
> // iroh reachability: bind a key, not a place. The endpoint publishes its
> // identity, and partners dial the id from anywhere.
> const server = listenIroh(Reachability.Discovery);
> console.log(server.uri); // ocapn://<node-id>.iroh?…; hand this to partners
> for await (const session of server) {
>   session.publish("greeter", (_verb, args) => `hello, ${args[0]}`);
>   session.serve().catch(() => {});
> }
> ```

Over iroh there is no host and port to bind. `listenIroh` takes that same
reachability posture, and the server reports its own locator through `server.uri`.

## Async

Sessions return native promises, and a session is disposable: `using session =
await connect(...)` closes it when the block ends, in place of an explicit
`session.close()`. For how one call can pipeline on another before it resolves,
see [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')).

## Actors

An actor is a callback, `(verb, args) => AppValue`, that you register with
`session.spawn` or `session.publish`; it returns any value you can send, and the
partner receives that as the reply. A throw becomes the caller's broken promise.
To hand one actor across an introduction so a third peer can reach it directly,
publish it on a `Host` with `publishGiftable`. You hold a `Host` as `server.host`
from `listen`, or mint one with `new Host()`. The `Host` is the multi-session
product in [modules]($link.page('modules')), and
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains the three-party handoff a giftable
rides on.

## Binding check and example

```sh
zig build deno-dogfood    # the Deno consumer against the pancake archives
zig build bun-dogfood     # the same source, on Bun
```

The full surface lives in the
[TypeScript binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/README.md).
The same source runs under Deno and Bun, and its wasm build is
[In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')).
